-- sl01: The measurement loop: process to operator display
insert into public.lessons (id, owner_id, path_id, position, title, objective, est_minutes, script, script_version, source_note, review_status, origin) values ('sl01', null, 'ccst-l1', 1, 'The measurement loop: process to operator display', 'Trace a basic measurement from the process to the operator display and name the major points where evidence can be checked.', 6, 'A measurement loop turns a physical condition into information someone can use. Think of it as a path with several handoffs.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

First is the process itself: pressure, temperature, level, or flow. A sensor responds to that condition. A transmitter converts the sensor response into a signal that can travel through wiring to a control-system input. The input converts the electrical signal into a value. The controller or display then presents that value to the operator.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Here is the path: process condition, sensing element, transmitter, field wiring, input channel, configured scaling, and display. A fault at any handoff can make the displayed value wrong, frozen, noisy, or absent.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

When a reading looks wrong, do not jump straight to replacing the instrument. Compare what you can observe at each point, using approved procedures and appropriate test equipment. Does the local indication agree with the control-room value? Is the transmitter output plausible? Does the input channel show a matching signal? Is the configured range consistent with the instrument range?

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

The key idea is localization. Each comparison narrows the fault to a section of the path. Never bypass required isolation, permits, or site safety rules to collect a measurement.', 1, 'DRAFT — TECHNICAL REVIEW REQUIRED. This lesson is original educational draft content and has not been technically validated. Do not publish, sell, or use it as approved training until a qualified instrumentation and controls reviewer verifies the technical details, answer key, and safety language.

Review note: A local indicator is not guaranteed to be independent of the transmitter or sensor; equipment design varies. Verify the actual loop drawing and device arrangement.', 'draft', 'seed')
