-- sl02: What a 4–20 mA signal tells you
insert into public.lessons (id, owner_id, path_id, position, title, objective, est_minutes, script, script_version, source_note, review_status, origin) values ('sl02', null, 'ccst-l1', 2, 'What a 4–20 mA signal tells you', 'Explain the meaning of live zero and calculate a linear signal value.', 6, 'In a common analog instrument loop, four milliamps represents the bottom of the configured measurement range and twenty milliamps represents the top. The signal is often called live zero because a valid low-end measurement is above zero milliamps.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

That offset can help distinguish a valid low reading from some faults, but it does not diagnose a fault by itself. A broken wire, a device fault, or a system-specific alarm may produce different behavior. Always check the instrument and input-channel documentation.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

For a linear range, convert the measured current to a fraction of span. Subtract four milliamps, then divide by sixteen milliamps. That gives the fraction from zero to one. Multiply by the configured engineering-unit span and add the lower range value.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Example: a zero-to-one-hundred psi range at twelve milliamps. Twelve minus four is eight. Eight divided by sixteen is one-half. Half of the range is fifty psi.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

The relationship is: process value equals lower range value plus current minus four, divided by sixteen, times span. Confirm the configured range and signal type before using the result. A device may be configured for square-root extraction or another transfer function, so do not assume every loop is linear.', 1, 'DRAFT — TECHNICAL REVIEW REQUIRED. This lesson is original educational draft content and has not been technically validated. Do not publish, sell, or use it as approved training until a qualified instrumentation and controls reviewer verifies the technical details, answer key, and safety language.', 'draft', 'seed')
