-- sl05: RTD versus thermocouple
insert into public.lessons (id, owner_id, path_id, position, title, objective, est_minutes, script, script_version, source_note, review_status, origin) values ('sl05', null, 'ccst-l1', 5, 'RTD versus thermocouple', 'Describe the basic signal behavior of an RTD and thermocouple and why wiring details matter.', 6, 'RTDs and thermocouples both measure temperature, but they produce different electrical behavior. An RTD changes electrical resistance as temperature changes. A transmitter or measurement circuit uses that resistance relationship to calculate temperature.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

A thermocouple uses two dissimilar conductors joined together. A temperature difference creates a small voltage. The measurement system uses that voltage, along with reference-junction compensation, to estimate temperature.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

The practical difference is that an RTD is fundamentally a resistance measurement, while a thermocouple is fundamentally a small-voltage measurement. Lead-wire arrangement, sensor type, polarity, extension wire, and input configuration matter. Exact details depend on the sensor and instrument model.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

If a temperature value is noisy or implausible, compare the installed sensor type with the configured input type. Then check drawings, terminations, and approved diagnostics. Do not assume a sensor is defective just because the display is wrong; configuration and wiring can create similar symptoms.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

For this lesson, remember the core distinction: RTD means changing resistance; thermocouple means a small voltage related to temperature difference. Use the manufacturer’s documentation for sensor curves, wire colors, and terminal details.', 1, 'DRAFT — TECHNICAL REVIEW REQUIRED. This lesson is original educational draft content and has not been technically validated. Do not publish, sell, or use it as approved training until a qualified instrumentation and controls reviewer verifies the technical details, answer key, and safety language.', 'draft', 'seed')
