-- sl06: Calibration versus verification
insert into public.lessons (id, owner_id, path_id, position, title, objective, est_minutes, script, script_version, source_note, review_status, origin) values ('sl06', null, 'ccst-l1', 6, 'Calibration versus verification', 'Distinguish checking an instrument from adjusting it and explain why as-found results matter.', 6, 'Verification asks whether an instrument performs within an accepted tolerance under stated test conditions. Calibration is the comparison of an instrument’s response with a known reference. Depending on the result and procedure, calibration work may include adjustment.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

A useful record separates the as-found condition from the as-left condition. As-found means what the instrument did before adjustment. As-left means what it did after any approved adjustment. If you record only the final result, you lose evidence about the condition discovered in the field.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

The reference used for comparison should be suitable for the task and handled under the applicable quality process. Record the range, test points, readings, tolerance or acceptance criterion, equipment identification, and result as required by the procedure.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Do not adjust an instrument simply because a value differs from expectation. First verify the correct range, procedure, reference, environmental conditions, and measurement setup. Then follow the approved work instruction and authorization.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

The practical memory aid is: verify means determine whether it meets criteria; calibrate means compare with a reference and, when authorized, adjust. Preserve both the condition found and the condition left.', 1, 'DRAFT — TECHNICAL REVIEW REQUIRED. This lesson is original educational draft content and has not been technically validated. Do not publish, sell, or use it as approved training until a qualified instrumentation and controls reviewer verifies the technical details, answer key, and safety language.', 'draft', 'seed')
