-- sl10: Close the work order with useful records
insert into public.lessons (id, owner_id, path_id, position, title, objective, est_minutes, script, script_version, source_note, review_status, origin) values ('sl10', null, 'ccst-l1', 10, 'Close the work order with useful records', 'Identify the details that make a maintenance record useful for traceability and future troubleshooting.', 6, 'A maintenance record should let the next person understand what was reported, what was observed, what work was done, and what condition remained afterward. A short entry can be useful if it is specific.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Record the equipment or loop identification, the original symptom, relevant operating conditions, checks performed, measured values when applicable, parts or settings changed, the result, and any follow-up needed. Include dates and required references to calibration equipment or procedures when the work requires them.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Separate fact from interpretation. “Input remained at 12.0 mA during the approved test” is an observation. “Transmitter is bad” is a conclusion and should be supported by evidence. If the cause remains uncertain, say so and record the next action.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Good records protect continuity. They help detect repeat failures, show the condition before and after work, and make handoff clearer. Follow your site’s required forms, naming conventions, approvals, and record-retention rules.

[PAUSE 3 SECONDS]

Before closing, ask: can another technician understand what happened without asking me to reconstruct it from memory? If not, add the missing observation or follow-up detail.', 1, 'DRAFT — TECHNICAL REVIEW REQUIRED. This lesson is original educational draft content and has not been technically validated. Do not publish, sell, or use it as approved training until a qualified instrumentation and controls reviewer verifies the technical details, answer key, and safety language.', 'draft', 'seed')
